Name: Rinnie (Corrinna Desert)

Race: Human
Age=12
Role: Dusty's younger sister. Lives in Aethres under the care of a healer as part of Dusty's enrollment deal.
Mana: No glow.  A cup.

Appearance:
Small and thin for her age. Black hair, black eyes, pale skin, facial features are soft. Dark circles under her eyes that never fully fade. Skin has improved since arriving in Aethres. The healer has brought color back, a faint warmth beneath the pallor.

Background:
Cared for by Dusty since their parents died to a disease.  Lived in the Stumps(economical homeless zone).  Dusty provided food for her and kept her sheltered.  

Overall systemic weakness with no identified cause. Chronic lethargy, limited stamina, requires frequent and extended rest. The healer in Aethres have improved her condition but have not diagnosed the root. They manage symptoms: fatigue, pallor, episodes of trembling when she overextends. She has good days and bad days. On good days she can tend her plants for a few hours before her hands shake. On bad days she does not leave her bed.

No one knows what it is. The healers have theories — mana deficiency, blood weakness, something congenital — but nothing has held under examination. The illness simply is, and Rinnie has lived inside it for so long she has stopped expecting it to have a name.

Personality
Genuinely sweet. Believes the best in people — not from ignorance (Stumps-raised), but because kindness costs nothing and means something. Learned from watching Dusty be gentle while starving.

Quirky. Mind wanders to odd places, shares without embarrassment. Names her plants, talks to them like a gardener. Finds strange connections between unrelated things and states them plainly.

Puts on a brave face for Dusty every visit — sits straighter, deflects to his life, perfects the reassuring smile. Knows he almost sees through it. Does it anyway: his worry costs more than her pretending.

Feels like a burden — quiet certainty, not self-pity. Knows everything Dusty sacrifices is for her. Carries it silently because telling him would only add weight.

Gratitude in action, not words: pressed flowers, remembering every detail, asking about people by name from a single mention visits ago.

Loves plants — the one thing her body reliably allows. Gets to be protector instead of protected.

Internal Thoughts:

Rinnie thinks at the pace her body allows — unhurried, drifting from one thing to the next in chains that make sense to her but look random from outside. She notices details before assigning meaning and doesn't mind if the meaning never comes. She thinks about people in specifics, not summaries — not "she's kind" but the small unnecessary thing they did. She holds contradictions without needing to pick one. Thoughts loop back later with slightly more weight. Her mind is the part of her that isn't tired, so it wanders — strange, quiet, gentle directions. The sad thoughts sit underneath and only surface at trigger points: her hands shaking, hearing Dusty coming and fixing her face. Otherwise she's just observing. Quietly. Everything.


Speech:

Plain words, short sentences. Not childish — just economical. She grew up with no room for excess. Occasionally something oddly precise slips through from years of watching instead of doing.

Questions are her main tool. She asks about other people to show care and to steer attention away from herself. "Did your mom like rain too, or just you?" Specific, small, remembered from a single mention.

With plants she's unguarded — bossy, funny, a little weird. That's where the real voice lives. "You're leaning again. The sun is over there."

Quirky observations come out flat and undecorated. "Clouds and bread dough look the same when they're not done yet." No punchline. No request for a reaction. Just how she sees things.

Humor is dry and small. "The healer says I need more sunlight. I told her I'm not a fern."
